    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "nums = [10, 2]            ->  \"210\"\nnums = [3, 30, 34, 5, 9]  ->  \"9534330\"\nnums = [0, 0]             ->  \"0\""
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea: compare by concatenation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Numeric order doesn't work (9 must come before 30), and neither does plain string order (\"3\" vs \"30\" is ambiguous). The right question for any two values a and b is: **which order of the two makes the bigger number?** Compare the strings `a + b` and `b + a` — they have the same length, so comparing them as strings compares them as numbers. Put `a` first if `a + b` is bigger."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This comparison is consistent (transitive), so a normal sort with it produces an order where **every neighbouring pair is in its best order** — and that is the largest number overall. One last detail: if the biggest piece is `\"0\"`, every value is 0, and the answer is `\"0\"`, not `\"000\"`."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Brute force: try every order"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function largestNumber(nums) {\n  const strs = nums.map(String);\n  let best = \"\";\n  const used = new Array(strs.length).fill(false);\n  const build = (cur, count) => {\n    if (count === strs.length) {\n      if (cur > best) best = cur;               // same length, so string order is number order\n      return;\n    }\n    for (let i = 0; i < strs.length; i++) {\n      if (used[i]) continue;\n      used[i] = true;\n      build(cur + strs[i], count + 1);\n      used[i] = false;\n    }\n  };\n  build(\"\", 0);\n  return best[0] === \"0\" ? \"0\" : best;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "from itertools import permutations\n\ndef largest_number(nums):\n    strs = [str(x) for x in nums]\n    best = max(\"\".join(p) for p in permutations(strs))   # same length, so string order is number order\n    return \"0\" if best[0] == \"0\" else best"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "String best;\n\nString largestNumber(int[] nums) {\n    String[] strs = Arrays.stream(nums).mapToObj(String::valueOf).toArray(String[]::new);\n    best = \"\";\n    build(strs, new boolean[strs.length], \"\", 0);\n    return best.charAt(0) == '0' ? \"0\" : best;\n}\n\nvoid build(String[] strs, boolean[] used, String cur, int count) {\n    if (count == strs.length) {\n        if (cur.compareTo(best) > 0) best = cur;      // same length, so string order is number order\n        return;\n    }\n    for (int i = 0; i < strs.length; i++) {\n        if (used[i]) continue;\n        used[i] = true;\n        build(strs, used, cur + strs[i], count + 1);\n        used[i] = false;\n    }\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "string best;\n\nvoid build(vector<string>& strs, vector<bool>& used, const string& cur, size_t count) {\n    if (count == strs.size()) {\n        if (cur > best) best = cur;                   // same length, so string order is number order\n        return;\n    }\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < strs.size(); i++) {\n        if (used[i]) continue;\n        used[i] = true;\n        build(strs, used, cur + strs[i], count + 1);\n        used[i] = false;\n    }\n}\n\nstring largestNumber(vector<int>& nums) {\n    vector<string> strs;\n    for (int x : nums) strs.push_back(to_string(x));\n    best = \"\";\n    vector<bool> used(strs.size(), false);\n    build(strs, used, \"\", 0);\n    return best[0] == '0' ? \"0\" : best;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n! · n·k), space O(n·k).** Every one of the n! orders is built and compared — fine for 8 values, hopeless for 100."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Sort with the concatenation comparator"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "largest-number",
      "caption": "Each comparison glues the two values both ways; the bigger result decides the order."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function largestNumber(nums) {\n  const strs = nums.map(String);\n  strs.sort((a, b) => (b + a > a + b ? 1 : b + a < a + b ? -1 : 0));   // a first if a+b is the bigger number\n  if (strs[0] === \"0\") return \"0\";                                     // every value was 0\n  return strs.join(\"\");\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "from functools import cmp_to_key\n\ndef largest_number(nums):\n    strs = [str(x) for x in nums]\n    strs.sort(key=cmp_to_key(lambda a, b: (b + a > a + b) - (b + a < a + b)))   # a first if a+b is bigger\n    if strs[0] == \"0\":\n        return \"0\"                        # every value was 0\n    return \"\".join(strs)"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "String largestNumber(int[] nums) {\n    String[] strs = Arrays.stream(nums).mapToObj(String::valueOf).toArray(String[]::new);\n    Arrays.sort(strs, (a, b) -> (b + a).compareTo(a + b));   // a first if a+b is the bigger number\n    if (strs[0].equals(\"0\")) return \"0\";                     // every value was 0\n    return String.join(\"\", strs);\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "string largestNumber(vector<int>& nums) {\n    vector<string> strs;\n    for (int x : nums) strs.push_back(to_string(x));\n    sort(strs.begin(), strs.end(), [](const string& a, const string& b) { return a + b > b + a; });   // a first if a+b is bigger\n    if (strs[0] == \"0\") return \"0\";                          // every value was 0\n    string out;\n    for (auto& s : strs) out += s;\n    return out;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n log n · k), space O(n·k)** for numbers of up to k digits: each comparison builds two strings of length up to 2k."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
